/*
 * The instances the compiler generates - direction 5 of `role.h`.
 *
 * The class is the library's - `class Num(a)` is written in `lib/Core`, joined by user types the
 * ordinary way - and what the compiler supplies is *instances of it* at types no source body could
 * implement: `Num(I32)`'s `+` is one machine instruction, and the conversion ladders are one
 * instance per ordered pair of widths.
 *
 * A table rather than a generator called from wherever an instance lookup happened to fail, which is
 * what this replaces: the current resolver asks four different files whether a failed lookup is one
 * of theirs, and the question "is this a class a vector could join" is asked at every lookup that
 * found nothing.
 *
 * ## Why the heads stay in the compiler
 *
 * Asked, and decided against writing them in source with intrinsic members. The instance *sets* are
 * target-dependent and quadratic: `Widen` and `Narrow` are one instance per ordered pair of the
 * widths a target has, so source would be generated source with the generator in the compiler
 * anyway - the same fact in two places, with the copy in the library where a reader would trust it.
 * What moves here is the naming: which class, and which family answers for it.
 *
 * ## What a generated instance is
 *
 * An ordinary `instance` declaration in Core's module, with one ordinary member function per class
 * member: a real signature, a real symbol, and a body the compiler writes. Nothing below the
 * resolver can tell it from one an author wrote - selection finds it in the same table, a generic
 * body reaches it through the same witness, and a `Function` with a body is what the erased ABI
 * hands a pointer to. What the compiler adds is that a member of one **expands where it is called**
 * when the call is concrete, so `a + b` at two `I32`s is the `add` node and never a call - which is
 * the property `compiler/resolve`'s `Emit` hooks existed for, and here it is one rule in `finishCall`
 * rather than a flag per method.
 *
 * The emitters are one stage's - `compiler/resolve_mir/intrinsic/expand_provided.cpp` - and they
 * index a class's members by **position**, which is the dependency `known.def` states for every
 * class row: what `Num`'s third member computes is a fact about the library's order, written once
 * there and read here.
 *
 * ## The columns
 *
 *  - `role` is the enumerator, and the order of the enum - which is also the order the instances
 *    are generated in, so a class whose superclass instance a generated head needs (`Num` needs
 *    `FromInt`, `Ord` needs `Eq`, `Integral` needs `Num` and `Bitwise`) comes after it.
 *  - `klass` is the `Known` role naming the class this generates instances of.
 *  - `family` is which types get one - `Family`, which is where the sets are argued.
 *  - `heads` is whether a head is one type of that family or a *pair* of them - `Heads`. The
 *    conversion classes relate two types, so their instance sets are the family's ordered pairs
 *    split by `intrinsic/convert.h`'s rule; every other row is one head per type.
 */

/*
 * What a literal is at a primitive - `1` at every number, `1.5` at the two floats.
 *
 * First, because `Num` declares `FromInt` as a superclass: `1` has to mean something for a type
 * before `+` on that type can be told what `x + 1` is. A generated `fromInt` **folds** a constant
 * argument to a constant of its own type, which is how a literal at `I32` stays `const 1` and never
 * a call; with a non-constant argument it is the conversion, which is what a generic body's `x + 1`
 * reaches once `a` is a width.
 */
//                  role         klass               family           heads
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( fromInt,     Known::fromInt,     Family::Numbers, Heads::Each )
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( fromDecimal, Known::fromDecimal, Family::Floats,  Heads::Each )

/*
 * The comparisons. `Eq` over everything a machine compares bit for bit and the two library enums
 * the compiler itself builds values of - `Bool`, because a condition is one, and `Ordering`,
 * because `compare` answers one and `Ord`'s defaults test it with `==`. `Ord` over the numbers
 * alone: a `Bool` has no order the language states, and `Ordering`'s would be a statement about
 * three words.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( eq,          Known::eq,          Family::Comparable, Heads::Each )
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( ord,         Known::ord,         Family::Numbers,    Heads::Each )

/*
 * Arithmetic over every number, and the bit operations over every integer - plus `Bool`, whose
 * `and`, `or`, `xor` and `not` are the logical connectives on a one-bit value. `Integral` is the
 * integers alone: a remainder and a shift mean nothing at a float or at a truth value.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( num,         Known::num,         Family::Numbers,  Heads::Each )
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( bitwise,     Known::bitwise,     Family::Bits,     Heads::Each )
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( integral,    Known::integral,    Family::Integers, Heads::Each )

/*
 * What a condition means - `Bool`'s instance is the identity and expands to nothing, and a number
 * is truthy when it is not zero, which is `doc/spec/expressions.md`'s rule and makes `NaN` truthy.
 * `Truth` is deliberately not generated for `Ordering`: the spec says which types have one, and a
 * three-valued enum is not among them.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( truth,       Known::truth,       Family::Truthy, Heads::Each )

/*
 * The bit counts - `Core.Bits`, whose five members are three instructions, an instruction the
 * feature levels differ about, and a subtraction.
 *
 * The class stays source, which is the half worth keeping: a newtype over a word is a plausible
 * instance of it. What is generated are the *bodies*, which as source would have been the five-step
 * SWAR fold and two smear chains that every backend then has to recognize again to reach the
 * instruction it already has.
 *
 * `Family::Machine` and not `Integers`, which is the whole content of this row beyond its name: a
 * narrow value is some bits of a wider register, so a count of a `U8`'s *storage* and a count of its
 * value are different numbers, and the widths where they are the same number are the widths the
 * machine counts in. See `hasMachineWidth`, which is that set said once for this row and for
 * `mulHigh`.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( bits,        Known::bits,        Family::Machine, Heads::Each )

/*
 * The byte reversal - `Core.ByteSwap`, one instruction per width.
 *
 * The class stays source for `Bits`' reason and the same half is worth keeping: a newtype over a
 * word is a plausible instance of it, and an author's own type may join. What is generated are the
 * *bodies*, which as source are the shift-and-mask trees `lib/Core/Bits.yana` keeps as a comment -
 * eleven instructions at sixty-four bits for something every machine here does in one, and a shape
 * the optimizer may reassociate before a peephole finds it.
 *
 * `Family::Swappable` and not `Machine`, which is the whole content of this row beyond its name: a
 * count reads a value and needs a width the machine counts in, and a reversal reads a value's
 * *bytes*, of which a sixteen-bit value has two whatever register holds it. See `hasByteOrder`,
 * which is that set said once.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( byteSwap,    Known::byteSwap,    Family::Swappable, Heads::Each )

/*
 * The bit permutations - `Core.BitPermute`, x86's `pext` and `pdep`.
 *
 * The class stays source for `Bits`' reason, and what is generated are the two bodies - which as
 * source are a loop over the mask's set bits, and which no backend could then recognize as the pair
 * of instructions it has. `lower/inst.def` is where what a target without them spends is written.
 *
 * `Family::Machine`, which is `Bits`' set and for its reason said at a different operation: a gather
 * reads the *value's* bits at the mask's positions, so a narrow value would be answering about the
 * register's padding. `lib/Core/Bits.yana` says the same thing as "instanced at 32 and 64 bits,
 * which is `Bits`' set".
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( bitPermute,  Known::bitPermute,  Family::Machine,   Heads::Each )

/*
 * The conversion ladders - one instance per ordered *pair* of numbers, split three ways.
 *
 * The only rows whose heads take two types, and the reason `Heads` is a column: `Widen` and `Narrow`
 * are one set of pairs divided by one predicate, and `Bitcast` is a different set drawn from the
 * same family. Which pair is which is `intrinsic/convert.h` and nothing here - this table says
 * *that* the compiler supplies them and over what, and that file says which class each pair joins.
 *
 * `Family::Numbers` for all three, which is where the sets come from: every integer the target has
 * and both floats. The ladder is therefore about a hundred and ninety instances against the twelve
 * a single-headed row makes, and every one of them expands at a concrete call site to the one
 * `convert` or `bitcast` node it is - see `expand_provided.cpp`. What is *not* generated is a rung
 * for a pair the rule relates by neither, which is a pair no conversion is written between at all
 * rather than one that narrows.
 *
 * Widening before narrowing before reinterpretation, which is the order they are argued in and
 * carries no dependency: none of the three has a superclass, so unlike `Num` needing `FromInt` this
 * order is a reading order alone.
 */
//                  role         klass               family           heads
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( widen,       Known::widen,       Family::Numbers, Heads::Widening    )
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( narrow,      Known::narrow,      Family::Numbers, Heads::Narrowing   )
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( bitcast,     Known::bitcast,     Family::Numbers, Heads::Reinterpret )

/*
 * Comparing two vectors, and choosing between two by the answer - Design-Vector §3.2.
 *
 * `Family::Shapes` because the heads are not a list: there is one, `Lanewise(Vec(a, n), Mask(a, n))`,
 * and `provideVectorInstances` is what generates it. What the row is *for* is the emitters - a
 * generated member is reached by `(role, position)`, and this class needs a role of its own where a
 * pointer's `Eq` reuses one.
 *
 * The **scalar** instances beside it - `Lanewise(a, Bool)` at every number - are the row below,
 * which is a second set of one class rather than more heads of this one.
 */
//                  role         klass               family          heads
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( lanewise,    Known::lanewise,    Family::Shapes, Heads::Each )

/*
 * And the same class over a **scalar**, whose mask is `Bool` - the one-lane case.
 *
 * A second row of one class, which nothing else here needs and this does: the vector heads are a
 * generator's and these are a family's, so they are two instance *sets* and the table is a table of
 * sets. Both name `Known::lanewise`, and `expand_provided.cpp` answers them with one arm - a
 * comparison and a select are the same nodes at one lane as at sixteen.
 *
 * What asks for it is the thing this family exists to make possible: an approximation written once
 * and instanced for `Float`, `Double` and `Vec(Float)` alike - `lib/Math`'s `Real`, which is where
 * the requirement that found this is written. Such a body has to choose between two values by a
 * comparison, and the only spelling that covers both shapes is `select(x .< y, a, b)`: `if` cannot,
 * because a vector comparison answers a mask and a condition wants a `Bool`. Without these
 * instances the shared body is expressible for the vector instances and not for the scalar ones,
 * which is exactly the split a class removes.
 *
 * Numbers only. `Bool` and a record are comparable and neither is a lane of anything, so a
 * `Lanewise` over one would be a second spelling of `Eq` with nothing asking for it.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( lanewiseScalar, Known::lanewise, Family::Numbers, Heads::Masked )

/*
 * What number and what word a payload-free sum is - `Core.Enum`, one instance per `data`.
 *
 * `Family::Shapes` for `Lanewise`'s reason at a different kind of head: "every payload-free sum in
 * the program" is not an enumerable set of primitives, it is a walk over the declarations - so the
 * heads come from a generator, `provideEnumInstances`, and what this row is for is the *emitters*,
 * which are reached by `(role, position)`.
 *
 * Generated eagerly, after every declaration is read, rather than on a failed lookup, which is how
 * `compiler/resolve` generated one until J173 deleted it. What either arrangement has to get right
 * is that a **declared** instance wins: here the walk skips a head the table already holds, and a
 * program may not write one anyway - which is the class's own ruling and not this table's.
 *
 * Last in the table because nothing depends on it and it depends on everything: an enum's `Eq` and
 * `Ord` are the *comparison* rows above at a head this walk supplies, so the primitives' instances
 * are in place before a single `data` is looked at.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( enumeration, Known::enumeration, Family::Shapes, Heads::Each )

/*
 * What a payload-free sum *reads* as - `Core.Show`, one instance per `data`, over the same heads.
 *
 * The one text form a constructor with no payload has is its name, which is what a derived `Show`
 * prints in every language that has one and what every enum in this tree that wrote a `Show` wrote
 * by hand. So the body is `Enum.nameOf` handed to the string sink, and that is why this row is
 * after `enumeration` as well as at the end: the instance it calls is generated by the same walk.
 *
 * `Known::show` is `Optional`, so a library that declares no `Show` generates none of these and a
 * program built against it says `show` about nothing. `stringPush` is optional beside it and is
 * checked at the same point, because a `show` that appends nothing is worse than no instance.
 *
 * A **declared** instance still wins, by `provideEnumInstances`' own rule: `Show(Bool)` is written in
 * `lib/Core/Show.yana` and is what a `Bool` gets.
 */
YANA_PROVIDED_ROLE( show, Known::show, Family::Shapes, Heads::Each )
